Glossary 

Indian Act: The primary law the Canadian government uses to administer Indian status, local First Nations governments, and the management of reserve land. A major federal law originally created to manage Indigenous people. While it is the only law that officially recognizes special rights and land, it was originally a tool for assimilation.

Assimilation: The process of forcing a group (Indigenous people) to give up their language, culture, and traditions to become exactly like the larger, more powerful group (European-Canadians).

Status Indian: A person who is legally recognized as "Indian" under the Indian Act, which gives them certain rights but also subjects them to government rules.

Indian Reserve: A specific area of land held by the Crown (government) for the use and benefit of an Indian Band.
Sedentary Lifestyle: A way of life where a group stays in one permanent place; the government used reserves to end traditional nomadic or seasonal movement.

The Pass System: A rule (now removed) that prevented Status Indians from leaving their reserve without written permission from a government agent.

Enfranchisement: A process where a Status Indian gave up their legal rights forever to become a "full" Canadian citizen. To be enfranchised, they had to meet "white standards," like becoming a lawyer or a farmer.

Self-Government: When a community has the power to make its own decisions, manage its land and money, and choose its own leaders without an outside government having total control.

Veto Power: Historically, a local federal agent had the power to "veto" (cancel) any decision made by a First Nations community, meaning the community couldn't truly govern itself.

Treaty Obligations: Legally binding promises made between the government and First Nations; some argue reserves must be kept to ensure the government fulfills these promises.

Aboriginal Rights: Inherent rights practiced by Indigenous peoples long before European contact, such as the right to hunt and fish for food.

Treaty Rights: Specific rights (like the right to trade for a "moderate livelihood") that were written into formal agreements between the British Crown and Indigenous Nations. These are considered by the Supreme Court of Canada to be part of Canadian law.

Sparrow Test: A legal rule used by courts to decide if the government is allowed to limit an Aboriginal right. For a limit to be legal, the government must show it has a good reason (like conservation) and has put Indigenous needs first.

Moderate Livelihood: A term from the Marshall case (Leo’s project) meaning that Mi'kmaq people have the right to fish and trade to earn a basic living (food, clothes, and housing), but not to build massive wealth. (also know “the 1999 Marshall case”)


